  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>SimLocal</strong> if you want a plan that comes with a real phone number already built in, and you'd rather have a no-questions refund if it doesn't work out. Pick <strong>eSIM Card</strong> if you want to size a plan to the exact GB and day count you need and are willing to pay separately for a number. Both are marketplace-style sellers covering a wide range of countries; the actual difference is in what's bundled versus sold as an add-on.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: A Bundled Number or a Paid Add-On</h2>
  <p>
    Most SimLocal UK plans include a full UK phone number for calls and texts alongside the data, confirmed by buying one ourselves. eSIM Card doesn't bundle a number into its data plans at all; instead it sells a separate virtual phone number add-on, starting around $2.40, through the same app as its regular data eSIMs. If having a real local number matters to your trip, SimLocal gets you there without an extra purchase for UK travel specifically; eSIM Card gets you there everywhere else, but it costs more on top of the data plan.
  </p>
  <p>
    The two also split on how precisely you can size a plan. eSIM Card's United States page alone lists roughly 30 combinations across seven fixed-data tiers (1GB through 100GB) at validity windows from 3 to 90 days, plus two separate unlimited tiers. SimLocal sells country, regional, and student-specific plans, but not that same granular per-day, per-GB matrix; it's built more around picking a destination and a bundle size than dialing in an exact data-and-day combination.
  </p>

