  </div>

  <h2>Is SimLocal or eSIM Card the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with SimLocal if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You're heading to the UK and want a real local number for calls and texts, not just data</li>
        <li>You want a no-quibble refund if the eSIM doesn't work out</li>
        <li>You'd rather message support on WhatsApp than dig through a help widget</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with eSIM Card if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You know your exact data need and trip length and want a plan sized to match</li>
        <li>You want the option to add a virtual number or a voice-enabled eSIM outside the UK</li>
        <li>You're confident about the plan before buying, since there's little room to change your mind after install</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Refund policies run almost opposite.</strong> SimLocal advertises a "No Quibble Refunds" policy: get your money back if you change your mind, no explanation required. eSIM Card's own policy page describes eSIMs as fundamentally non-refundable once installed or used, with exceptions limited to narrow cases like a genuine coverage failure, claimed within 30 days. If there's any chance you'll want to back out, that difference alone could decide it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>SimLocal has a small physical footprint eSIM Card doesn't.</strong> SimLocal runs a handful of staffed airport counters, including one we've seen ourselves at Heathrow, on top of its app and website. eSIM Card, like most providers on this site, is app-and-browser only. If you'd rather have someone walk you through setup in person, that's a real point in SimLocal's favor, though only near one of its limited locations.
  </p>
  <p>
    If neither fits, <a href="/alosim/">AloSIM</a> bundles a free Hushed-based international number into its plans at no extra cost, a third approach worth a look if a phone number matters but you're not headed to the UK.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>SimLocal vs eSIM Card FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, SimLocal or eSIM Card</h3>
      <p>They're not priced on the same scale: eSIM Card lists granular per-GB, per-day US pricing starting under $2 for a small bundle, while SimLocal prices per destination and plan rather than a flat per-GB matrix. For UK trips specifically, SimLocal's bundled phone number can make it the better value once you account for what a separate number would cost elsewhere.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either one give me a real phone number</h3>
      <p>SimLocal does, as part of most UK plans, confirmed by buying one ourselves. eSIM Card sells a number as a separate paid add-on, starting around $2.40, rather than bundling it into the data plan.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which has the better refund policy</h3>
      <p>SimLocal, by a wide margin. Its "No Quibble Refunds" policy gives your money back if you change your mind, no explanation required. eSIM Card's policy treats eSIMs as non-refundable once installed or used, with only narrow exceptions.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Can I buy either one in person at an airport</h3>
      <p>SimLocal runs a small number of staffed airport counters on top of its app and website. eSIM Card is app-and-browser only, like most providers on this site.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

